Here is a minimal Express/Node.js server setup that you can copy into a file called server.js and run with node once you have the dependencies installed.

1. Install the required packages (run this in your project folder):

```
npm init -y
npm install express
```

2. Create a file named server.js with the following content:

```
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// Middleware to parse JSON bodies
app.use(express.json());

// Example route
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello, world! The server is running.');
});

// Start the server
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

3. Start the server:

```
node server.js
```

You should see a message in the console that says “Server listening on port 3000”. Visiting http://localhost:3000/ in a browser will display “Hello, world! The server is running.” You can add more routes, middleware, or integrate databases as needed.

Here’s a very simple Express server that listens on port 3000 and has one route that returns a friendly greeting:

```js
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// Simple route that sends back a greeting
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello from Express!');
});

// Start the server
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server is running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

To run it:

1. Install Express if you haven’t already: `npm install express`.
2. Save the code to a file named `server.js`.
3. Start the server with `node server.js`.
4. Open a browser and go to `http://localhost:3000/` to see the greeting.
